      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U04-L17-ACTIVITY-007",
      "SourceSection": "17.2: How Long Will It Take?",
      "SourceExcerpt": "Explain why we can write \\(h = \\log_{2}{x}\\) to represent the number of hours, \\(h\\), it takes for the one thousand bacteria to reach a population of \\(x\\) thousand.",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u04-l17.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U04-L17-ACTIVITY-008",
      "SourceSection": "17.2: How Long Will It Take?",
      "SourceExcerpt": "Complete the table with the corresponding values of \\(h\\).",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u04-l17.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U04-L17-ACTIVITY-009",
      "SourceSection": "17.2: How Long Will It Take?",
      "SourceExcerpt": "Plot the pairs of values on the coordinate plane. Make two observations about the graph.",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u04-l17.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U04-L17-ACTIVITY-010",
      "SourceSection": "17.2: How Long Will It Take?",
      "SourceExcerpt": "Use the graph to estimate the missing values in the table.",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u04-l17.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U04-L17-ACTIVITY-011",
      "SourceSection": "17.3: Another Logarithmic Function",
      "SourceExcerpt": "Earlier we saw that \\(h = \\log_2 x\\) represents the number of hours for 1 thousand bacteria, doubling every hour, to reach a population of \\(x\\), in thousands.",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u04-l17.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U04-L17-ACTIVITY-012",
      "SourceSection": "17.3: Another Logarithmic Function",
      "SourceExcerpt": "Suppose the function \\(d\\), defined by \\(d(x) = \\log_{10} x\\), represents the number of days it takes 1 thousand of another species of bacteria to reach a population of \\(x\\), in thousands. How is this population of bacteria growing?",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u04-l17.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U04-L17-ACTIVITY-013",
      "SourceSection": "17.3: Another Logarithmic Function",
      "SourceExcerpt": "Graph \\(d\\) using graphing technology. Make two observations about the graph.",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u04-l17.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U04-L17-ACTIVITY-014",
      "SourceSection": "17.3: Another Logarithmic Function",
      "SourceExcerpt": "Use your graph to estimate the values of \\(d(50)\\) and \\(d(20,\\!000)\\). (Adjust your graphing window as needed.) Explain what each value means in this situation.",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u04-l17.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U04-L17-ACTIVITY-015",
      "SourceSection": "17.3: Another Logarithmic Function",
      "SourceExcerpt": "Estimate or find the population after 5 days.",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u04-l17.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U04-L17-ACTIVITY-016",
      "SourceSection": "17.3: Another Logarithmic Function",
      "SourceExcerpt": "Without graphing, how do you think the graphs of the equations \\(y =\\log_2(x)\\) and \\(y = \\log_{10}(x)\\) compare? Do they ever meet?",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u04-l17.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U04-L17-ACTIVITY-017",
      "SourceSection": "17.3: Another Logarithmic Function",
      "SourceExcerpt": "Graph both equations on the same axes to test your conjectures.",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u04-l17.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U04-L17-ACTIVITY-018",
      "SourceSection": "17.3: Another Logarithmic Function",
      "SourceExcerpt": "Earlier we have studied exponential relationships where the input values are the exponent in the function. Sometimes we want to express an exponential relationship where the values we want to find, the outputs, are the exponents. A…",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u04-l17.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U04-L17-ACTIVITY-019",
      "SourceSection": "17.3: Another Logarithmic Function",
      "SourceExcerpt": "For example: Suppose the population of a town starts at one thousand and doubles every decade since first measured. We can write \\(P = 1 \\boldcdot 2^d\\) or \\(P = 2^d\\) to represent the population, in thousands, after \\(d\\) decades.",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u04-l17.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U04-L17-ACTIVITY-020",
      "SourceSection": "17.3: Another Logarithmic Function",
      "SourceExcerpt": "But if we want to know how long, in decades, it would take to reach certain population sizes, in thousands, we can write a logarithmic function \\(\\displaystyle d = \\log_2 P\\). In this function, the input is \\(P\\), population in thousands,…",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u04-l17.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U04-L17-ACTIVITY-021",
      "SourceSection": "17.3: Another Logarithmic Function",
      "SourceExcerpt": "We can use the graph to estimate the answer to a question such as, “How many decades will it take for the population to reach a million?” In this case, the answer is about 10 decades, because one million is 1,000 thousands and \\(\\log_2…",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u04-l17.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U04-L17-ACTIVITY-022",
      "SourceSection": "17.3: Another Logarithmic Function",
      "SourceExcerpt": "Suppose the population of that town expands by a factor of 10 every decade instead of by a factor of 2. The function representing the time it takes to reach a certain population, in thousands, would be \\(\\displaystyle d = \\log_{10} P\\).",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u04-l17.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U04-L17-ACTIVITY-023",
      "SourceSection": "17.3: Another Logarithmic Function",
      "SourceExcerpt": "From the graph, we can see that it takes only 3 decades to reach 1,000 thousands, because \\(\\log_{10} 1,\\!000=3\\) (or \\(10^3 = 1,\\!000\\)).",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u04-l17.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U04-L17-ACTIVITY-024",
      "SourceSection": "17.3: Another Logarithmic Function",
      "SourceExcerpt": "A logarithmic function is a constant multiple of a logarithm to some base, so it is a function given by \\(f(x) = k \\log_{a}(x)\\) where \\(k\\) is any number and \\(a\\) is a positive number (10, 2, or \\(e\\) in this course). The graph of a…",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u04-l17.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U04-L17-ACTIVITY-025",
      "SourceSection": "17.3: Another Logarithmic Function",
      "SourceExcerpt": "logarithmic function",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u04-l17.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
